/// Chapter 29 of the guide, written from md-docs/learn/16-testing.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page TestingGuide(path: "/docs/guide/testing", title: "Testing", description: "How to write wf tests: render tests with snapshots, seeded stores, tests that click and type in headless Chrome, wf verify, and running them in CI.", layout: DocsShell("Testing", kind: .tutorial, chapter: t("nav.group.quality"), blurb: "Tests written in WebFluent: render a component and read what it shows, or click through it in a real browser — then check every page in one.", source: "md-docs/learn/16-testing.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "write-a-test-that-looks", label: "Write a test that looks"), Anchor(id: "write-a-test-that-acts", label: "Write a test that acts"), Anchor(id: "seed-data-and-stores", label: "Seed data and stores"), Anchor(id: "test-a-whole-page", label: "Test a whole page"), Anchor(id: "run-the-tests", label: "Run the tests"), Anchor(id: "check-every-page-with-wf-verify", label: "Check every page with wf verify"), Anchor(id: "run-the-checks-in-ci", label: "Run the checks in CI"), Anchor(id: "choose-what-to-test", label: "Choose what to test"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/data", prevLabel: t("ch.24"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/security", nextLabel: t("ch.30"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to test a WebFluent project: render a component and check what it shows, click and type through a page in a real browser, and load every page of the built site to catch errors before a reader does.\n\nYou write tests in WebFluent itself, as `test \"name\" \{ … \}` declarations. A test renders something — a component, a fragment of a page, a whole page — and holds it to what it must and must not show. A test that clicks or types runs in a headless browser. `wf verify` then loads every page of the built site in one.")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("- A project with at least one component or page to test.\n- For a test that clicks, types or presses a key, and for `wf verify`: Chrome or Chromium on the machine, or the `WF_CHROME` environment variable pointing at one. A test that only looks needs neither.")

        Heading("Write a test that looks", id: "write-a-test-that-looks").h2

        Markdown("To check what a component shows, place it in a test and say what the text must and must not contain. Put tests in `tests/*.wf`, or beside the code in `src/`. A test has the project's components, stores, types and constants at hand.\n\nThis file declares a price tag and three tests of it:")

        CodeBlock(#"component PriceTag(_ amount: Number, sale: Bool = false) {
    Row(gap: .sm, align: .center) {
        Text(format(amount, .currency)).bold
        if sale { Badge("Sale").danger }
    }
}

test "a price tag shows the amount as currency" {
    PriceTag(1234.5)
    expect "$1,234.50"
    expect not "Sale"
}

test "a sale tag carries the badge" {
    PriceTag(10, sale: true)
    expect "Sale"
}

test "a list renders each row"(data: { rows: [{ name: "Ada" }, { name: "Grace" }] }) {
    for r in rows { Text(r.name) }
    expect "Ada"
    expect "Grace"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Run `wf test`, and each test prints a line; all three pass. The parts of a test that looks:\n\n- `expect \"text\"` — the text must appear in what the page shows. `expect not \"text\"` — it must not.\n- `data: \{ … \}` — values the body reads, by name. A `state` in the body starts with its initial value.\n- `t(\"key\")` — reads the project's translations, in its default locale.")

        Heading("How expect reads the page", id: "how-expect-reads-the-page").h3

        Markdown("`expect` reads the text a reader sees, never the markup: a class name in the HTML does not count. It reads the text laid out as a browser lays it out:\n\n- runs of white space are one space;\n- a block, or anything a `Row`, `Stack` or `Grid` lays out, is set apart from what is beside it;\n- inline elements that touch read as touching, so `Text \{ Code(\"live\") Code(\": \") Code(\"[Deploy]\") \}` reads `live: [Deploy]`.\n\nThe line breaks and indentation of the snapshot are not text. A render and a browser read the same page the same way.\n\nIn a browser, a page may still be moving — a row on its way out, a fetch on its way in. `expect` reads the page again until it holds, for up to a second and a half.")

        Heading("Snapshots", id: "snapshots").h3

        Markdown("Each render is also compared to a *snapshot*, the HTML saved in `tests/__snapshots__/<file>/<test>.html`. `wf test` writes a snapshot when it is missing and fails, with a diff, when a render no longer matches it. After a change you meant, run `wf test --update` to rewrite them.")

        Heading("Write a test that acts", id: "write-a-test-that-acts").h2

        Markdown("To check what a click, a keystroke or typed text does, add steps that act. A click cannot be rendered — it runs a handler, which runs an action, which changes a store, which repaints — so a test with an action in it is compiled into a real page, served, and run in a headless browser. It uses the same compiler, the same runtime and the same output a reader gets.\n\nThis test types an incident, reports it, and checks the count goes up:")

        CodeBlock(#"store IncidentStore {
    state titles: [String] = ["Disk full", "Login slow"]
    derived open = titles.length
    action report(title: String) { titles = titles.concat([title]) }
}

test "reporting an incident opens one more" {
    use IncidentStore
    state draft = ""
    Text("{IncidentStore.open} open")
    Input(bind: draft, label: "What happened")
    Button("Report") { on click { IncidentStore.report(draft) } }

    expect "2 open"
    type "Queue backing up" into "What happened"
    click "Report"
    expect "3 open"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The steps run **in the order you write them**. That order is the test: what a click did is visible only in the `expect` that follows it.")

        Table(caption: "Write a test that acts") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Step").header
                    Table.Cell("What it does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`expect \"text\"` · `expect not \"text\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("What the page must, or must not, show")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`click \"Save\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("Clicks whatever carries that name: a button or a link, a control, a tab, a menu's item, an option, a row of a tree")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`type \"Ada\" into \"Name\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("Types into the control that label names")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`press \"Enter\"` · `press \"Escape\" in \"Search\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A key, on the focused element or a named one; `Escape` closes an open `Modal` or `Dialog` as a real one would") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("A step finds what it acts on the way a reader does: by the name on it. A button's name is its text; a control's is its label, its placeholder or its `aria-label`. The accessibility checks hold a page to the same names ([Accessibility](/docs/guide/accessibility)), so a test that passes here is a page somebody can use.")

        Callout("**A handler that throws fails the test**, even when every `expect` would have passed. A click that quietly did nothing is the bug, not a pass.", kind: .important)

        Markdown("A test that acts takes no snapshot: its expects are the test.")

        Heading("The page a test that acts runs on", id: "the-page-a-test-that-acts-runs-on").h3

        Markdown("The page is drawn as the built site is:\n\n- it links the project's scripts under `src/`, so a click that calls one runs it;\n- it bundles the project's stylesheets under `src/` after the theme's rules and before the rules `style \{ \}` blocks compile to;\n- it links the stylesheets `meta.stylesheets` names;\n- it serves `public/` beside it.\n\nSo a rule that hides a word, or one that names a font or an image under `public/`, behaves the same in a test as on the site. A test that only looks renders without any of them.")

        Heading("Test a portal", id: "test-a-portal").h3

        Markdown("A `Portal` draws its block into a node that `into:` names, and only the running page draws it. So a test whose body holds a portal — or places a component that holds one — is compiled into a page and run in the browser too, with no snapshot:")

        CodeBlock(#"test "a portal draws into the element a handle names" {
    Stack(ref: spot)
    Portal(into: spot) { Text("in") }
    expect "in"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Seed data and stores", id: "seed-data-and-stores").h2

        Markdown("To start a test from a known state, name it in `data:`. A key that is a value's name supplies that value; a key that is a store's name seeds that store's state. Seeding works the same in a test that only looks and in one that clicks.\n\nThis test fills the cart with two of one item and checks the summary:")

        CodeBlock(#"type CartItem { id: String, qty: Number }

store Cart {
    state items: [CartItem] = []
    derived count = items.map(i => i.qty).sum()
}

component CartSummary {
    Text("{Cart.count} items")
}

test "the cart shows what is in it"(data: { Cart: { items: [{ id: "a", qty: 2 }] } }) {
    CartSummary()
    expect "2 items"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Test a whole page", id: "test-a-whole-page").h2

        Markdown("To click through a page as a reader does, place the page in a test by its name, as you would a component. This works for a page whose route takes no parameter. Its state, its stores and its handlers all run:")

        CodeBlock(#"test "a task is added with Enter" {
    Tasks
    type "Walk the dog" into "New task"
    press "Enter" in "New task"
    expect "Walk the dog"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`press \"Enter\"` in a form's text field submits the form, as a real key does. The `spa` and `static` projects that `wf init` writes carry tests like this under `tests/`; run `wf test` in a new project to see them pass.")

        Heading("Run the tests", id: "run-the-tests").h2

        Markdown("To run every test under `tests/`, and any declared in `src/`:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf test")
            ])

        Markdown("To run one file's tests, in the project it belongs to:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf test tests/cart.wf")
            ])

        Markdown("To rewrite the snapshots after a change you meant:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf test --update")
            ])

        Markdown("`wf test` prints a line per test and exits non-zero when one fails, so it works as a CI step as it is. A build never includes tests.")

        Heading("Check every page with wf verify", id: "check-every-page-with-wf-verify").h2

        Markdown("To catch what only a running page shows, build the site and load every page of it:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf build && wf verify")
            ])

        Markdown("`wf verify` serves the build and opens every route in headless Chrome. It fails on:\n\n- an uncaught exception or a `console.error`;\n- a request that failed, or an image that did not load;\n- a page that rendered no text.\n\nFor each route it prints the first contentful paint, the size and the requests. It also lists the built-ins no page drew — a fact about coverage, not a failure. Its options:\n\n- `--budget 400` fails a route that takes more than 400 ms to paint;\n- `--json` prints the report for a pipeline;\n- `--returning-visitor` visits every route again with storage holding what the previous build's pages kept.\n\n[CLI](/reference/cli#wf-verify) has the details.")

        Heading("Run the checks in CI", id: "run-the-checks-in-ci").h2

        Markdown("To run the formatter, the build, the tests and `wf verify` on every push, add a workflow such as this one to `.github/workflows/check.yml`:")

        CodeBlock(#"name: Check
on: [push, pull_request]
jobs:
  check:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | bash
      - run: echo "$HOME/.webfluent/bin" >> "$GITHUB_PATH"
      - run: wf fmt --check
      - run: wf build
      - run: wf test
      - run: wf verify --budget 1000"#, title: "yaml")

        Markdown("GitHub's Ubuntu runners come with Chrome, so the tests that act and `wf verify` run there as they do on your machine.")

        Heading("Choose what to test", id: "choose-what-to-test").h2

        Markdown("The type checker and the accessibility lints already catch a large class of mistakes. Tests are for the behaviour they cannot see:\n\n- **Components** with more than one state: each branch a prop can take.\n- **Stores**, through what a component shows of them, seeded with `data:`.\n- **Flows** a reader depends on — signing in, adding to a cart, submitting a form — as tests that act.\n- **Every page**, with `wf verify`, on every build.")

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Markdown("**A step cannot find what it names.** The test fails and prints what the page showed instead:")

        CodeBlock(#"  FAIL  tests/cart.wf — the count follows what is added
        nothing to click called "Publish"
        the page showed:
        Nothing here"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("Check that the name matches the button's text or the control's label, placeholder or `aria-label` exactly, and that an earlier step has drawn it.\n\n**A test that acts says there is no browser.** Install Chrome or Chromium, or set `WF_CHROME` to the path of one.\n\n**A snapshot test fails after a change you meant.** Run `wf test --update` and commit the new snapshots.")
    }
}
